tel 1.0

base capricious
import soundness

info   random values of any type, from an explicitly chosen source and an explicitly chosen distribution
detail
    _Capricious_ generates random values of any type — not just numbers — through the
    `Randomizable` typeclass, which is derived for case classes, enums and sealed hierarchies
    from their structure, so a whole tree of test data is one call. The *source* of randomness
    and the *shape* of the numbers are separate, explicit choices: the source is a
    `Randomization` imported by name from the `randomization` package — seeded and
    reproducible, unseeded, or cryptographically secure — and the shape of a `Double` is a
    `Distribution` in scope.

    A `Random` is a capability: drawing from a generator advances its state, so code which
    consumes randomness takes a `Random` as a contextual value, and `stochastic` opens a scope
    with one.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import randomization.seededRandomization
    import randomSizes.uniformSizeUpto100

    case class Point(x: Int, y: Int)

    given Seed = Seed(1L)

    stochastic:
      given Distribution = Gaussian(0.0, 2.0)
      (arbitrary[Int](), arbitrary[Point](), arbitrary[Double](), arbitrary[List[Int]]())
    ```

    Four choice packages hold givens which are imported by name, since `import soundness.*`
    does not import givens: `randomization` (the source), `randomDistributions` (the shape of a
    `Double`), `randomSizes` (the size of a random collection) and `randomTexts` (a source of
    awkward strings). The choice is always written at the import, so a simulation and a token
    generator cannot be confused.

    The library has two source sets:

    - `capricious.core` — everything but WASI's entropy source. Compiles for the JVM, Scala.js
      and Scala Native, over `java.util.Random` and `java.security.SecureRandom`.
    - `capricious.wasi` — `randomization.wasiRandomization`, which draws from the
      `wasi:random/random` interface of a WebAssembly component, with `WasiRandom` and the
      `wasiApis.wasiRandomApi` binding it needs.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and each choice package merges with the
    umbrella's own.

refer capricious.Random
refer capricious.Randomizable
refer capricious.stochastic
refer capricious.arbitrary
refer capricious.randomization
refer capricious.Distribution

entry .stochastic   runs a block with a fresh `Random`, from the `Randomization` in scope
    `stochastic(block)` initializes a new generator from the `Randomization` given — which must
    be imported by name from the `randomization` package — and runs `block` with it as its
    contextual `Random`. Under `seededRandomization`, with a `Seed` in scope, the whole block is
    a function of the seed, so a failing randomized test can be replayed by supplying the same
    seed again; `Seed#stochastic` is the same with its seed supplied.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import randomization.seededRandomization

    given Seed = Seed(42L)

    stochastic(List.fill(16)(arbitrary[Long]())) == stochastic(List.fill(16)(arbitrary[Long]()))
    ```

    Each `stochastic` block starts a new generator, so two blocks under the same seed draw the
    same sequence; successive draws *within* one block differ. The determinism is of the order
    of draws: concurrent strands drawing from one block interleave differently on each run, so a
    concurrent computation which must be reproducible needs a block, and a seed, per strand.

entry .arbitrary   a random value of any `Randomizable` type, from the `Random` in scope
    `arbitrary[T]()` is `Randomizable.apply` for `T`, drawing from the contextual `Random`,
    such as the one `stochastic` provides. A `Double` needs a `Distribution` in scope, and a
    collection a `Random.Size`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import randomization.unseededRandomization

    enum Colour:
      case Red, Green, Blue

    stochastic:
      (arbitrary[Int](), arbitrary[Boolean](), arbitrary[Colour]())
    ```

entry .random   a random value of any `Randomizable` type, from the shared global generator
    `random[T]()` draws from `Random.global`, needing no `Random`, `Randomization` or `Seed` in
    scope. It is for scripts and one-off values: the global generator is unseeded and shared,
    so nothing drawn from it can be reproduced. Code which should be testable takes a `Random`
    and uses `arbitrary`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    random[Long]()
    ```

entry .toss   a fair coin-toss: `true` or `false`, evenly
    `toss()` needs a `Random` in scope, as a mark that the code is stochastic, but draws from
    `scala.math.random`, the JDK's shared generator, rather than from that `Random`: a toss is
    therefore not reproducible from a `Seed`. For a reproducible coin, use `arbitrary[Boolean]()`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import randomization.unseededRandomization

    stochastic:
      toss()
    ```

entry .Random   a pseudorandom generator, as a capability whose state advances with each draw
  detail
      A `Random` wraps a `scala.util.Random`. It is a capability (`caps.Unscoped`), so code
      which draws from it carries it in its capture set, and is a `Findable`, so it is reached
      as a contextual value. It is made by `stochastic` or `Seed#stochastic` from the
      `Randomization` in scope, or reached directly as `Random.global`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomization.seededRandomization

      Seed(3L).stochastic:
        val random = summon[Random]
        (random.long(), random.unitInterval(), random.gaussian(), random[Int]())
      ```
  refer capricious.stochastic
  refer capricious.Randomization
  refer capricious.Randomizable
  entry .global   a `Random` over one shared, unseeded generator, for use with no scope
      Each call wraps the same process-wide generator, made lazily by
      `randomization.unseededRandomization`, so draws from it are never reproducible. `random`
      uses it.
  entry .apply   a new `Random` from the `Randomization` in scope
      `Random(seed)` initializes a generator from the contextual `Randomization`. The `seed`
      argument is not passed to it: a seeded generator takes its seed from the `Seed` given
      which `seededRandomization` itself requires, so `Seed#stochastic` is the reliable way to
      seed a scope.
  entry .Size   how many elements a random collection has, as a contextual value
    detail
        A single-method trait: `generate(random)` draws a size. `Randomizable.list`,
        `Randomizable.set` and `Randomizable.frozenArray` need one, and the `randomSizes`
        package holds uniform sizes up to 10, 100, 1000, 10000 and 100000, imported by name.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import randomization.seededRandomization

        given Random.Size = _ => 5

        Seed(1L).stochastic(arbitrary[List[Int]]())
        ```
    entry #generate   draws a size from a `Random`
  entry #long   draws a uniformly random `Long`
  entry #gaussian   draws a `Double` from the standard normal distribution: mean 0, deviation 1
  entry #unitInterval   draws a `Double` uniformly from `0.0` inclusive to `1.0` exclusive
  entry #apply   draws a value of any `Randomizable` type: `random[Point]()`
  entry #shuffle   a `List` with its elements in a random order, drawn from this generator
      Reproducible under a seed: the same seed shuffles the same list the same way.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomization.unseededRandomization

      stochastic:
        summon[Random].shuffle(List(1, 2, 3, 4, 5))
      ```

entry .Randomizable   the typeclass of types whose values can be drawn at random, as `T is Randomizable`
  detail
      A single-method typeclass: `#randomize` takes a `Random` and draws a value. The
      companion has instances for `Long`, `Int`, `Short`, `Byte` and `Char` (each the low bits
      of a random `Long`), `Boolean` (the sign of one), `Seed`, `Double` (shaped by the
      `Distribution` in scope), `List`, `Set` and frozen arrays (sized by the `Random.Size` in
      scope), and a derivation: a case class of randomizable fields draws each field, and an
      enum or sealed hierarchy picks one of its variants uniformly and draws it. A type whose
      values need more care defines an instance, often by `#map`ping an existing one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomDistributions.uniformUnitIntervalDistribution

      case class Latitude(degrees: Double)

      given Latitude is Randomizable =
        summon[Double is Randomizable].map(unit => Latitude(unit*180 - 90))
      ```

      A derived instance draws its fields not from the `Random` it is given but from a new
      generator, initialized from the `Randomization` found where the instance is expanded.
      Under `seededRandomization` that generator restarts from the seed, so successive derived
      values in one `stochastic` block may come out alike.
  refer capricious.arbitrary
  refer capricious.Random
  refer capricious.Distribution
  entry #randomize   draws a value from a `Random`
  entry #apply   draws a value from the contextual `Random`: `summon[Int is Randomizable]()`
  entry #map   an instance for another type, transforming each drawn value
      `randomizable.map(lambda)` draws through this instance and applies the lambda; the
      result captures both.
  entry .long   draws a uniformly random `Long`
  entry .byte   draws a `Byte`, the low eight bits of a random `Long`
  entry .short   draws a `Short`, the low sixteen bits of a random `Long`
  entry .int   draws an `Int`, the low thirty-two bits of a random `Long`
  entry .char   draws a `Char`, the low sixteen bits of a random `Long`
      Any UTF-16 code unit, including unpaired surrogates and control characters.
  entry .seed   draws a `Seed` of sixty-four bits, from a random `Long`
  entry .boolean   draws a `Boolean`: `true` when a random `Long` is negative
  entry .list   draws a `List` whose size comes from the `Random.Size` in scope
  entry .set   draws a `Set` of up to the `Random.Size` in scope, duplicates collapsing
  entry .frozenArray   draws a frozen `Array[element]^{}` sized by the `Random.Size` in scope
      Needs a `ClassTag` for the element type as well as a `Random.Size`.
  entry .double   draws a `Double` through the `Distribution` in scope
      There is no default distribution: a `Double` cannot be drawn until the code says what
      kind of `Double` it means, which prevents treating a uniform draw as if it were normal.
      Import one from `randomDistributions` or declare a `Gaussian`, `PolarGaussian`,
      `UniformDistribution` or `Gamma` as a given.
  entry .conjunction   the derived instance of a product: each field drawn in turn
  entry .disjunction   the derived instance of a sum: a variant chosen uniformly, then drawn

entry .Randomization   a source of pseudorandom generators: seeded, unseeded or secure, chosen by import
  detail
      A single-method trait: `#initialize` makes a new `scala.util.Random`. `stochastic` needs
      one in scope, and the `randomization` package's givens are the standard choices, each
      imported by name. A `Findable`, so it is reached as a contextual value.
  refer capricious.randomization
  refer capricious.stochastic
  entry #initialize   makes a new generator

entry .Seed   the seed of a reproducible generator: some bytes, usually from a `Long`
  detail
      A case class of the seed's bytes as `Data`. `Seed(42L)` makes one from a `Long`'s eight
      bytes. A `Seed` in scope is what `randomization.seededRandomization` and
      `randomization.secureSeededRandomization` initialize their generators from.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomization.seededRandomization

      Seed(42L).stochastic(arbitrary[Long]()) == Seed(42L).stochastic(arbitrary[Long]())
      ```

      Two seeds from the same `Long` are not `==`, since `Data` is an array; compare them by
      `#long`.
  refer capricious.randomization.seededRandomization
  entry .apply   a seed of the eight bytes of a `Long`
  entry #value   the seed's bytes
  entry #entropy   the seed's size in bits: eight per byte
  entry #long   the seed's first eight bytes as a `Long`, recovering the `Long` it was made from
  entry #stochastic   runs a block with a `Random` seeded from this seed
      As `stochastic`, with this seed as the contextual `Seed`; a seeded `Randomization` must
      still be in scope, such as `randomization.seededRandomization`.

entry .Distribution   the shape a random `Double` is drawn from: Gaussian, uniform, gamma, …
  detail
      A single-method trait: `#transform` draws a `Double` from a `Random`. A random `Double`
      needs one in scope (`Randomizable.double`). `Gaussian`, `PolarGaussian`,
      `UniformDistribution` and `Gamma` are the built-in shapes, and `randomDistributions` has
      four ready-made givens.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomization.unseededRandomization

      stochastic:
        given Distribution = UniformDistribution(4.0, 6.0)
        List.fill(100)(arbitrary[Double]())
      ```
  refer capricious.randomDistributions
  entry #transform   draws a `Double` from a `Random`, in this distribution's shape

entry .Gaussian   the normal distribution of a mean and a standard deviation, by the Box–Muller transform
  detail
      A case class of `mean` (default 0) and `std` (default 1), drawing two uniform values from
      the unit interval for each sample. `PolarGaussian` is the same distribution by the polar
      method.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomization.seededRandomization

      Seed(1L).stochastic:
        given Distribution = Gaussian(0.0, 2.0)
        arbitrary[Double]()
      ```
  entry #mean   the distribution's mean
  entry #std   the distribution's standard deviation

entry .PolarGaussian   the normal distribution of a mean and a standard deviation, by the polar method
  detail
      A case class of `mean` (default 0) and `std` (default 1). The Marsaglia polar method draws
      pairs of values from the symmetric unit interval, rejecting those outside the unit circle,
      and so avoids the trigonometry of `Gaussian`'s Box–Muller transform.
  entry #mean   the distribution's mean
  entry #std   the distribution's standard deviation

entry .UniformDistribution   the flat distribution between a start and an end
  detail
      A case class of `start` and `end`: every value in the range is equally likely.
      `randomDistributions.uniformUnitIntervalDistribution` is the range 0 to 1 and
      `uniformSymmetricUnitIntervalDistribution` the range −1 to 1.
  entry #start   the lower end of the range
  entry #end   the upper end of the range

entry .Gamma   the gamma distribution of a shape and a scale, by the Marsaglia–Tsang method
  detail
      A case class of `#shape` (k) and `#scale` (θ). `Gamma.approximate(mean, std)` finds the
      shape and scale with a given mean and standard deviation, which suits a positive,
      right-skewed quantity such as a duration or a size.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomization.seededRandomization

      Seed(1L).stochastic:
        given distribution: Gamma = Gamma.approximate(100, 10)
        arbitrary[Double]()
      ```
  entry .approximate   the gamma distribution with a given mean and standard deviation
      The shape is mean² ÷ variance and the scale variance ÷ mean.
  entry #shape   the shape parameter, k
  entry #scale   the scale parameter, θ
  entry #mean   the distribution's mean, shape × scale, as an `F64`
  entry #variance   the distribution's variance, shape × scale², as an `F64`
  entry #variationCoefficient   the coefficient of variation, 1 ÷ √shape, as an `F64`
  entry #skewness   the distribution's skewness, 2 ÷ √shape, as an `F64`

entry .randomization   the standard sources of randomness, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `stochastic` needs a `Randomization` in scope, and none is there by default: the choice
      between a reproducible generator and an unpredictable one is always written at the
      import. `import soundness.*` does not import givens, so each is imported by name.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Seeded
        code
            import soundness.*
            import randomization.seededRandomization

            given Seed = Seed(42L)
            stochastic(arbitrary[Long]())   // the same Long on every run
      alternative Unseeded
        code
            import soundness.*
            import randomization.unseededRandomization

            stochastic(arbitrary[Long]())   // a different Long on every run
      alternative Secure
        code
            import soundness.*
            import randomization.secureUnseededRandomization

            stochastic(arbitrary[Long]())   // from the operating system's secure source
      ##
      ```
  entry .unseededRandomization   a fresh `java.util.Random` each time: fast, varying from run to run
  entry .secureUnseededRandomization   a `SecureRandom`, seeded by the platform, for values which must not be predicted
  entry .stronglySecureRandomization   `SecureRandom.getInstanceStrong`: the platform's strongest source, which may block
      Suited to long-lived keys; the strong source can block while the system gathers
      entropy, so it is not for bulk generation.
  entry .seededRandomization   a `java.util.Random` seeded from the `Seed` in scope: fully reproducible
      Needs a `Seed` given. Every generator it initializes starts from the same seed, so two
      `stochastic` blocks under it draw the same sequence.
  entry .secureSeededRandomization   a `SecureRandom` seeded from the `Seed` in scope
      Needs a `Seed` given. A `SecureRandom` may mix the seed with its own entropy, so on most
      platforms the sequence is not reproducible; use it to supplement a secure source, not to
      replay one.
  entry .wasiRandomization   draws from the WASI `wasi:random/random` interface of a Wasm component
      In `capricious.wasi`. An inline given, expanded where it is summoned, which calls
      `get-random-u64` through a `WasiRandom`; it needs `wasiApis.wasiRandomApi` in scope and
      works only in code compiled for a Wasm target.

entry .randomDistributions   ready-made distributions for random `Double`s, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A random `Double` needs a `Distribution` in scope. These cover the common cases; any
      `Gaussian`, `PolarGaussian`, `UniformDistribution` or `Gamma` can be declared as a given
      instead. `import soundness.*` does not import givens, so each is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomization.unseededRandomization
      import randomDistributions.gaussianDistribution

      stochastic(arbitrary[Double]())
      ```
  entry .gaussianDistribution   the standard normal distribution: mean 0, standard deviation 1
  entry .uniformUnitIntervalDistribution   uniform between 0 and 1
  entry .uniformSymmetricUnitIntervalDistribution   uniform between −1 and 1
  entry .binaryDistribution   a `Double` made from sixty-four random bits, of any magnitude

entry .randomSizes   uniform sizes for random collections, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A random `List`, `Set` or frozen array needs a `Random.Size` in scope. Each of these
      draws a size uniformly from zero up to, but not including, its bound. `import
      soundness.*` does not import givens, so each is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomization.seededRandomization
      import randomSizes.uniformSizeUpto100

      Seed(42L).stochastic(arbitrary[List[Int]]())
      ```
  entry .uniformSizeUpto10   a size from 0 to 9
  entry .uniformSizeUpto100   a size from 0 to 99
  entry .uniformSizeUpto1000   a size from 0 to 999
  entry .uniformSizeUpto10000   a size from 0 to 9999
  entry .uniformSizeUpto100000   a size from 0 to 99999

entry .randomTexts   random `Text`s for testing, each a given to import by name
  detail
      There is no default `Text is Randomizable`, since no one distribution of strings suits
      every test. `import soundness.*` does not import givens, so this is imported by name.
  entry .naughtyStringsText   draws from the Big List of Naughty Strings: text which often breaks parsers
      Each draw picks one line of the bundled Big List of Naughty Strings — reserved words,
      Unicode edge cases, injection attempts, unusual whitespace — read from the classpath
      resource `/capricious/blns.txt` when the given is first used.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import randomization.unseededRandomization
      import randomTexts.naughtyStringsText

      stochastic(arbitrary[Text]())
      ```

entry .WasiRandom   a `java.util.Random` driven by an external 64-bit entropy source, such as WASI's
    In `capricious.wasi`. `WasiRandom(entropy)` adapts a function returning random `Long`s to
    `java.util.Random`'s `next` contract, taking the top bits of each, so it can back a
    `scala.util.Random` and hence a `Randomization`. Its inherited seed is never used.
    `randomization.wasiRandomization` is built on it.

entry .WasiRandomApi   the type of the WIT interface `wasi:random/random`, as capricious binds it
    In `capricious.wasi`: an `Interface in Wit` typed by the WIT definitions bundled at
    `/capricious/random.wit`, which `randomization.wasiRandomization` calls through. The given
    is `wasiApis.wasiRandomApi`.

entry .wasiApis   the WIT interface bindings capricious needs on WASI, each a given to import by name
  detail
      In `capricious.wasi`. `randomization.wasiRandomization` is expanded where it is summoned,
      and needs this binding visible there. `import soundness.*` does not import givens, so it
      is imported by name.
  entry .wasiRandomApi   the `wasi:random/random` interface, read from `/capricious/random.wit`
